"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][15]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M15-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M15-L1 – Microcontrollers, Memory and Peripherals<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Embedded Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Digital\nlogic<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Storage\nelements<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Read a microcontroller\narchitecture and establish an explicit memory and computing budget for a\nsensor node.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a Program to an Embedded System<\/h2>\n<p>An embedded system is a computer whose function is integrated into a\nlarger engineering task. A microcontroller (MCU) combines a processor\ncore, program memory, working memory and peripherals on a single chip. A\nsensor device, for example, must not only calculate a measured value but\nalso read it within a specified time, check its validity and forward it.\nElectrical and timing behaviour are therefore part of the design.<\/p>\n<p>The processor core contains registers, an arithmetic unit, a control\nunit and a program counter. Flash retains instructions and constant data\neven without power; SRAM holds mutable data, the call stack and buffers.\nPeripherals perform time-critical tasks: timers count autonomously, GPIO\ncontrols and reads pins, an ADC digitises analogue inputs, and serial\ninterfaces transmit data. An interrupt controller relays events to the\nprocessor core. Direct memory access (DMA) can transfer data between\nperipherals and RAM without a CPU instruction for each byte.\nConfiguration, synchronisation and completion handling remain\nnecessary.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_052_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Block diagram of an MCU with CPU, flash, SRAM, bus, timer, interrupt controller and sensor/UART interfaces.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The processor and memories share the internal bus\nwith timers, sensor and communication peripherals; interrupts and DMA\ndecouple events from executing CPU code.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Address Space and Register Access<\/h2>\n<p>With memory-mapped I/O, control and status registers appear in the\nCPU address space. Reading a status register differs from reading SRAM:\nit can have hardware-dependent side effects. Address allocation and\npermitted write operations are defined by the datasheet, not by the C\nlanguage.<\/p>\n<p>For a fictional 32-bit control register with enable bit 3, the mask\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2^3=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an ordinary readable and writable register,\n<code>CTRL |= (1u &lt;&lt; 3)<\/code> sets the bit while preserving the\nother bits; <code>CTRL &amp;= ~(1u &lt;&lt; 3)<\/code> clears it. A\nwrite-one-to-clear status bit, however, is acknowledged by writing a\none. A read-modify-write operation could inadvertently clear additional\nbits there. With concurrent access, the entire read-modify-write\nsequence is not atomic even for an ordinary register.<\/p>\n<p>A <code>volatile<\/code>-qualified register access in C tells the\ncompiler that observable accesses must not simply be eliminated. It\nprovides neither safe data exchange between contexts nor general memory\nbarriers. Device headers and the manufacturer’s documented access rules\ntake precedence. The addresses of one MCU family cannot be transferred\nto arbitrary other controllers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Memory Budget of the Sensor Node<\/h2>\n<p>Throughout this module we design a fictional device with a 48 MHz CPU\nclock, 64 KiB of flash and 8 KiB of SRAM. These figures are model\nassumptions, not a specific product. A record occupies 12 bytes on the\ntransmission line (see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L4<\/a>);\nit is stored in the buffer as exactly 12 bytes, without struct padding.\nAll other memory items are budgeted separately.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>SRAM area<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Bytes<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Application and drivers, static data<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2048<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stack including nested interrupts (budget)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1536<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16 waiting records<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">192<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Active transmit buffer including auxiliary data<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">32<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnostic and receive buffers<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">512<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Queue counters and management data<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">64<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4384<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8192<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4384<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3808<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_{frei}=8192-4384=3808\\ \\mathrm{Byte}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here the subscript frei denotes free memory. With 32 waiting records\nthe total increases by 192 bytes to 4576 bytes. The budget is not a\nmeasurement of actual consumption: the linker script, map file,\nalignment, libraries, stack high-water mark and, where applicable, DMA\nmemory areas must be verified in the hardware design. For flash we\nassume 36 KiB of program memory including constants; 28 KiB remains for\nextensions and potential boot/update areas. An initialised global object\nmay occupy both flash (initial values) and SRAM (mutable runtime\ncopy).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computing Time and Design Decision<\/h2>\n<p>At 100 measurements per second and 48 MHz, ideally 480000 clock\ncycles are available per measurement interval. If the CPU portion\nrequires 100 µs, this corresponds to 4800 cycles or 1% of the available\ntime. The number of cycles is not an instruction count: pipelines,\nmemory buses, flash wait states and multi-cycle operations affect\nexecution time. The remaining time can be used for other tasks or sleep\nstates. However, sleep only reduces average power if the corresponding\nclock and power domains are actually switched off; wake-up latency and\nenergy must be considered.<\/p>\n<p>An average utilisation of 1% does not guarantee a deadline. If a\nlong, non-interruptible driver call starts just before the sampling\ndeadline, a measurement may start too late despite the CPU being mostly\nidle. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L2<\/a>\nwe therefore calculate with deadlines and maximum blocking times.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many additional bytes are required for 64 instead of 16\nrecords?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>48<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>576<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">48\\cdot12=576<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbytes, provided the queue management budget remains unchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why would it be incorrect to interpret the clock frequency directly\nas the number of C lines executable per second?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> The compiler and architecture translate a\nline into different instruction sequences; their execution time\nvaries.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A status register clears set flags when a one is written. How do you\nacknowledge bit 3 alone?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Under the hypothetical register contract,\nwrite only <code>1u &lt;&lt; 3<\/code>; the other bits are unaffected\nwhen written as zero. Do not use read-modify-write.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The map file shows 2600 rather than 2048 bytes of static data. Is\nthe budget sufficient for 16 records?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 4936 bytes in total, leaving 3256 bytes. Only\nstack and runtime measurements can further substantiate these\nassumptions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ee.ethz.ch/studies/bachelor/bsc-eeit/third-year/core-courses.html\">ETH\nZürich – Core Courses and Specialisations (Bachelor)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Core Courses:\nEmbedded Systems, Computers and Networks; curricular comparison, not\nevidence of the specific semester offering. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://arm-software.github.io/CMSIS_6/main/Core/device_h_pg.html\">Arm\nCMSIS-Core – Device Header File<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Interrupt Number Definition and\nDevice Peripheral Access Layer: interrupt numbers, register definitions\nand base addresses; rolling documentation, not pinned to v6.3.0. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://users.ece.utexas.edu/~valvano/EE445M/lectures.html\">University\nof Texas at Austin – Embedded and Real-Time Operating Systems, lecture\noverview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · ECE445M, lecture schedule week 1: microcontroller\narchitecture and peripherals; slides not reviewed. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M15-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M15-L2 – Clocks, Timers and Interrupts<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Embedded Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Microcontrollers<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">States\nand sampling<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Configure a periodic timer,\ninterpret sampling jitter and verify a deadline using conservative\nexecution-time bounds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Timers and Time Base<\/h2>\n<p>A hardware timer receives a clock signal and counts according to a\nconfigurable scheme. For an up-counter with prescaler register\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PSC<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand auto-reload register\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ARR<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nboth counted from zero, the interval is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=\\frac{(PSC+1)(ARR+1)}{f_{tim}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For an assumed timer clock of 48 MHz,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>47<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PSC=47<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9999<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ARR=9999<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive a counter clock of 1 MHz and an event after 10000 counts,\ni.e. every 10 ms. On real hardware the timer clock may differ from the\nCPU clock. When reconfiguring, buffer registers and update events must\nbe considered; the formula applies to steady-state periodic\noperation.<\/p>\n<p>Polling (regularly checking) detects an event only at the next\npolling instant. A perfect 1 ms polling period without blocking produces\na detection delay between zero and just under 1 ms. An interrupt signals\nit independently of the main program, but not necessarily immediately:\nmasking, higher-priority routines and hardware latency delay the\nstart.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sequence and Timing Terms<\/h2>\n<p>The release time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_k=kT<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the scheduled event, the start time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe actual start of work, and the completion time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe end of work. The response time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_k=f_k-r_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA relative deadline\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis met if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_k\\le D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere jitter describes variation in the actual start relative to the\nscheduled time. The periodicity of a hardware clock does not guarantee\nthat a sensor read by the main program is sampled periodically.<\/p>\n<p>In the sensor node, the timer instant is recorded as the timestamp of\nthe read request. It is not necessarily the physical sampling instant in\nthe sensor; that depends on when the sensor performed its internal\nconversion. If the sensor itself measures periodically, its data-ready\nsignal or an internal timestamp must be used if necessary.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_053_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Acquisition timeline showing the start phase, bus waiting time, processing, timeout and 2 ms deadline.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A normal acquisition and the timeout case are\ncompared on the same timeline against a relative 2 ms deadline; bus\nwaiting dominates the illustrated budget.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Conservative Timing Budget<\/h2>\n<p>We require at most 20 µs of CPU time for the start phase, an\nasynchronous bus operation normally taking 500 µs or a monitored waiting\nperiod of up to 1500 µs after the start phase, and 80 µs for completion\nprocessing and packet assembly. The undisturbed model takes 600 µs, and\nthe fault case takes 1600 µs. The release interval is 10 ms, and the\nacquisition/diagnostic deadline is 2 ms. UART transmission follows\nasynchronously and has its own deadline of 150 ms.<\/p>\n<p>For an extended hardware budget, allow at most 50 µs of start latency\nand a total of 120 µs of further delay due to higher-priority IRQs or\nrelease blocking during acquisition. Subject to these assumptions, which\nmust be demonstrated, we have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1500<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1770<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mathrm{max}}\\le 50+20+1500+80+120=1770\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}&lt;2000\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This bound makes no independent statement about hardware WCET\n(worst-case execution time). Without demonstrated execution-time and\nblocking bounds, it is merely a design budget. The final simulation in\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L5<\/a>\nuses ideal delays of 600 and 1600 µs; the additional disturbance times\nare not simulated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concurrency and Counter Wraparound<\/h2>\n<p>Interrupt service routines (ISRs) should remain short, time-bounded\nand free of blocking operations. They often flag an event or insert an\nitem into a queue; the main logic processes it. Disabling interrupts for\na long time can prevent certain data races but can itself cause missed\ndeadlines. The NVIC in Cortex-M handles prioritised interrupts; the\nnumber of interrupts and priority bits depends on the\nimplementation.<\/p>\n<p>A 32-bit counter ticking once per microsecond wraps around after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>32<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>71<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>58<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{32}\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}\\approx71{.}58<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nminutes. In unsigned 32-bit arithmetic the modular difference also gives\nelapsed time across one wraparound, provided the actual difference is\nsmaller than the full counter period. To check a deadline, compare the\nelapsed time since the start with the interval; a naive comparison\n<code>now &gt;= deadline<\/code> fails at wraparound.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>MASK <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bn\">0xffffffff<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>start <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bn\">0xfffffff0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>now <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bn\">0x00000020<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>elapsed <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (now <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> start) <span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span> MASK<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> elapsed <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">48<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>With a clock error of, for example, 50 ppm, a 1 s interval may differ\nby approximately 50 µs; over 1 h this amounts to about 0.18 s.\nIncreasing counter resolution does not eliminate this systematic\ndeviation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With the counter clock unchanged at 1 MHz, choose the register\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ARR<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor 5 ms.<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 4999.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In a fault case the additional delay increases from 120 to 400 µs.\nIs the 2 ms deadline sufficient?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> The upper bound rises to 2050 µs. Meeting the\ndeadline is no longer demonstrated, even if individual measurements\nfinish on time.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A serial interface transmits while the CPU performs other tasks. Is\nthe transmission duration CPU computing time?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> No. Only servicing the interface consumes CPU\ntime; data transmission occupies a separate resource.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a timestamp taken when UART data arrives not reliably\nidentify the measurement instant?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Processing, queue waiting time and\ntransmission delay the arrival by varying amounts. The measurement\nrequest or sensor timestamp must be stored separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://users.ece.utexas.edu/~valvano/EE445M/lectures.html\">University\nof Texas at Austin – Embedded and Real-Time Operating Systems, lecture\noverview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · ECE445M, lecture schedule weeks 1 and 4: timers and\ntiming; slides not reviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://arm-software.github.io/CMSIS_6/main/Core/group__NVIC__gr.html\">Arm\nCMSIS-Core – Interrupts and Exceptions (NVIC)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · NVIC API: IRQn,\nenabling and disabling interrupts; partial scope. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M15-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M15-L3 – Serial Interfaces and Sensor Interfacing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Embedded Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Timers and\ninterrupts<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">CMOS\nlevels<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare UART, SPI and I²C by\ntransmission method and application limitations, and calculate data\nrates and electrical connection requirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three Transmission Methods<\/h2>\n<p>For an asynchronous UART connection, the transmitter and receiver\nagree on the bit rate. An 8N1 frame comprises a start bit, eight data\nbits (least significant bit first), no parity bit and one stop bit.\nThere is no separate clock line. Bidirectional operation usually\nrequires TX, RX and a common ground reference. UART describes the data\nframe, not automatically the electrical levels of RS-232 or RS-485;\nthese may require transceivers.<\/p>\n<p>SPI is clock-synchronous. A controller generates SCK; separate data\nlines allow simultaneous transmission and reception in the usual\nfour-wire arrangement. Chip select selects a target. Clock polarity CPOL\nand clock phase CPHA determine the idle state and sampling edge,\nrespectively. Both devices must use the same mode according to their\ndatasheets. SPI has no general bus standard for device-level addresses,\nacknowledgement or frame length; these rules depend on the sensor.<\/p>\n<p>With conventional I²C, several devices share SDA (data) and SCL\n(clock), typically with open-drain outputs and pull-ups. The address\nselects the target. Each eight bits are followed by an acknowledgement\nbit. A target can delay the clock signal if clock stretching is\nsupported. START or STOP is indicated by an SDA level transition while\nSCL is high. Address conflicts, bus loading, pull-up resistances and\npotentially competing controllers must be checked.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property<\/th>\n<th>UART<\/th>\n<th>SPI<\/th>\n<th>I²C<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clock line<\/td>\n<td>none<\/td>\n<td>SCK<\/td>\n<td>SCL<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Addressing<\/td>\n<td>not in UART<\/td>\n<td>usually chip select<\/td>\n<td>in bus protocol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data direction<\/td>\n<td>usually TX/RX<\/td>\n<td>usually separate lines<\/td>\n<td>shared SDA<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical local task<\/td>\n<td>diagnostics, data stream<\/td>\n<td>fast ADC, flash<\/td>\n<td>multiple sensors<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Reading an I²C Sensor Register<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional sensor uses a 7-bit address <code>0x48<\/code> and an\n8-bit register <code>0x10<\/code> for two measurement data bytes. The\naddress bytes on the bus are <code>0x90<\/code> when writing and\n<code>0x91<\/code> when reading. Software libraries require either the\nunshifted address or an already shifted address byte, depending on the\nlibrary. The two must not be confused.<\/p>\n<p>The transaction consists of START, address+W, register byte, repeated\nSTART, address+R, first data byte (the controller acknowledges with\nACK), second data byte (the controller responds with NACK) and STOP.\nEach of the five bytes, including the address bytes, requires nine clock\ncycles including ACK/NACK. Minimum at 100 kHz:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>100000<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>450<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{bus}=\\frac{5\\cdot9}{100000}=450\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With a modelled 50 µs for START/STOP/bus-free times and driver\ntransitions, we budget 500 µs without stretching. At 100 measurements/s\nthis is 5% of bus time. At 400 kHz the 45 clocks would ideally take\n112.5 µs; the additional budget of 50 µs must be verified independently.\nSupport for the higher rate does not follow from this calculation\nalone.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_054_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"I²C read transaction with five nine-bit groups, and comparison of UART transmission times at 9600 and 19200 bit/s.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Five I²C byte groups of nine clocks each yield a\nnominal bus time of 450 µs at 100 kHz; UART transmission time decreases\nwhen the bit rate doubles.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Electrical Limit of the I²C Bus<\/h2>\n<p>For an RC pull-up model with bus capacitance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe voltage after releasing the line is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(t)=V_{DD}(1-e^{-t/(R_pC_b)})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe rise time between 30 and 70% is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>8473<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r=R_p C_b\\ln\\frac{0{.}7}{0{.}3}\\approx0{.}8473R_pC_b.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At 3.3 V, 100 pF and 4.7 kΩ, the result is approximately 398 ns. This\nis below the 1000 ns limit for Standard-mode, but above 300 ns for\nFast-mode. With 3.3 kΩ the result is about 280 ns. If the device can\nreliably sink at least 3 mA at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OL}=0{.}4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV, the current budget gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>003<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>967<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{min}=(3{.}3-0{.}4)/0{.}003\\approx967\\ \\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor Fast-mode at 100 pF,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3541<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{max}\\approx3541\\ \\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe choice of 3.3 kΩ lies within this interval but has limited margin\nfor capacitance and resistance tolerances. This does not replace\nchecking component specifications and actual signal edges.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>UART Throughput and Comparison with SPI<\/h2>\n<p>A 12-byte packet with 8N1 occupies 120 bit periods. Its transmission\ntakes 6.25 ms at 19200 bit/s and 12.5 ms at 9600 bit/s. Transmitting 100\npackets/s requires at least 12000 bit/s without pauses. Utilisation at\n19200 is 62.5%; at 9600 it is 125%. No buffer depth can compensate for a\npermanently inadequate data rate.<\/p>\n<p>A hypothetical SPI sensor that uses one command byte followed by two\ndata bytes requires at least 24 µs at 1 MHz plus chip-select and driver\ntimes. This is faster than the I²C example but uses additional pins and\na device-specific frame. A bus comparison must consider sensor\nconversion time, electrical topology, energy and interference immunity\nas well as throughput.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many 8N1 payload bytes per second can 19200 bit/s theoretically\ncarry?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 1920 bytes/s. Application headers and\nchecksums must be counted in this total.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The bus capacitance increases to 200 pF. What is the rise time with\n3.3 kΩ?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> About 559 ns; too long for Fast-mode in this\nmodel.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An SPI controller transmits in mode 0, but the sensor expects mode\n3. Is the same frequency sufficient?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> No. The clock edges and idle state do not\nmatch the agreed interface.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the I²C controller acknowledge the last byte read with\nNACK?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> It signals the end of the read operation to\nthe transmitting target before generating STOP.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/user-guide/UM10204.pdf\">NXP –\nUM10204, I²C-bus specification, Rev. 7.0, §§ 3.1, 6, 7.2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · UM10204,\nRev. 7.0, p. 9, §3.1.4–3.1.5: start/stop conditions and byte format;\npartial scope. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onlinedocs.microchip.com/oxy/GUID-6F1B86DC-C230-43D1-A405-0CC2E7EDF7EE-en-US-2/GUID-EAC2D943-F395-4D47-98C8-4EEBC798BFA2.html\">Microchip\n– SPI Timing, CPOL and CPHA<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · AVR151, §1.4, Table 1-3: SPI modes and\nclock edges. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onlinedocs.microchip.com/oxy/GUID-D5344754-46CE-40D6-918B-245670AF7E39-en-US-6/GUID-0DB53D39-B94A-4512-95BD-D6BA10D41F83.html\">Microchip\n– USART Overview, asynchronous operation and 8N1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2 Overview:\nasynchronous 8N1 frame and baud rate; device-specific manual requires\nseparate review. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M15-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M15-L4 – Embedded Software as a State Machine<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Embedded Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Digital\nstorage<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Interfaces<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define non-blocking acquisition\nlogic with a timeout, a data format and a bounded FIFO.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Firmware as Coordination of Independent Tasks<\/h2>\n<p>Embedded firmware connects hardware drivers, application decisions\nand data transport. If these functions are mixed in a single blocking\nloop, the measurement period depends on the transmission duration and\nbus faults. Clear separation allows the next measurement request to\nstart independently while UART transmission is in progress.<\/p>\n<p>The driver handles registers, bus accesses and hardware\nnotifications. The application logic processes states, thresholds and\nfaults. A transmission component manages the queue. The three components\ncommunicate through defined events and data objects. The state machine\nadvances when an event arrives, rather than by continually waiting in a\nloop.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<th>Action and next state<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ready<\/td>\n<td>Timer<\/td>\n<td>Record sequence and time, start read → Waiting<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waiting<\/td>\n<td>Bus success<\/td>\n<td>Accept measured value → Packet assembly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waiting<\/td>\n<td>Bus fault or timeout<\/td>\n<td>Set fault status, mark measured value invalid → Packet assembly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Packet assembly<\/td>\n<td>Queue has space<\/td>\n<td>Append packet → Ready<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Packet assembly<\/td>\n<td>Queue full<\/td>\n<td>Drop new packet, increment loss counter → Ready<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waiting<\/td>\n<td>New timer event (overrun)<\/td>\n<td>Count missed request, do not overwrite ongoing request<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_055_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Acquisition state machine with a timeout path and bounded transmit queue, separate from the UART transmit buffer.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Acquisition and UART transmission operate\nseparately; when the FIFO is full, a new packet is discarded and\ncounted, while a packet already being transmitted occupies no FIFO\nslot.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Successful completion and timeout must unambiguously identify the\nsame request. On cancellation, the driver is reset or placed in a\ndefined fault state; late completion notifications for the cancelled\nrequest must not generate a second record. Actual I²C bus recovery is\ndevice-dependent. In the case study, a timeout is abstractly modelled as\na completed failed read; no electrical bus-recovery algorithm is\nclaimed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ring Buffer and Overload<\/h2>\n<p>A FIFO returns items in insertion order. A ring buffer with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphysical slots also stores the occupancy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhere, so that all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nslots are usable. Write index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand read index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadvance modulo\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter each successful access. The invariants are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le n\\le N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=(r+n)\\bmod N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor transmission, a complete record is moved from the queue into the\nseparate active transmit buffer. This active record does not count\ntowards queue occupancy.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">class<\/span> Ring:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">__init__<\/span>(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>, size):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> size <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.buf <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>size<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.r <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.w <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.n <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> push(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>, x):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.n <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.buf):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.buf[<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.w] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> x<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.w <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.w<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">%<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.buf)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.n <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> pop(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.n:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.buf[<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.r]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.r <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.r<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">%<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.buf)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.n <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> x<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> Ring(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> q.push(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> q.push(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> q.push(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> q.push(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> q.pop() <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> q.push(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> [q.pop(), q.pop(), q.pop(), q.pop()] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>This code is sequential. In actual firmware, the ISR and main program\nmust access indices and occupancy consistently; a suitable short\ncritical section or a proven single-producer/single-consumer method is\nrequired. <code>volatile<\/code> alone is insufficient. If a DMA transfer\nis started, its source must not be overwritten until completion is\nconfirmed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An Explicit Data Format<\/h2>\n<p>A packet is exactly twelve bytes regardless of the compiler;\nmulti-byte numbers are serialised in little-endian order. The sequence\ncounter increments at every scheduled request, even if the packet is\nsubsequently dropped. A sensor fault is valid diagnostic information,\nnot a numerical measurement.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Byte offset<\/th>\n<th>Content<\/th>\n<th>Format<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0–1<\/td>\n<td>Sync word<\/td>\n<td><code>AA 55<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2–3<\/td>\n<td>Sequence<\/td>\n<td>unsigned 16-bit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4–7<\/td>\n<td>Request time<\/td>\n<td>unsigned 32-bit, µs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8–9<\/td>\n<td>Raw value<\/td>\n<td>unsigned 16-bit; <code>FFFF<\/code> on fault<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td>Status<\/td>\n<td>0 valid, 1 timeout<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<td>CRC-8<\/td>\n<td>polynomial 0x07, init 0, MSB first, XOR-out 0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The CRC is calculated over the first eleven bytes, including the sync\nword. For the ASCII bytes <code>123456789<\/code>, this variant produces\n<code>F4<\/code>. CRC is an error-detection mechanism, not cryptographic\nauthentication or a guarantee against all multi-bit errors. The sync\nword can also appear within a payload field. If alignment is lost, a\nreceiver must find candidates for the packet boundary, check length and\nCRC, and continue searching if a candidate is rejected; the sequence\ncounter helps with plausibility checking. Comparisons across counter\nwraparound use modular arithmetic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which Faults Must Be Distinguished?<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasurement requests/s and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbytes per frame, the average generation rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=B f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA transmission pause of 120 ms at 100 Hz can produce approximately\ntwelve newly completed packets. Depending on the phase and an already\noccupied queue, more items may be waiting. The capacity limit must\ntherefore be checked against the intended load, not just the average\ndata rate.<\/p>\n<p>Three diagnostics are maintained separately: sensor timeout (packet\nwith status 1), queue overflow (packet loss, local counter), and\ntransmission error (receiver-side CRC/sequence checks). An actual\nreceiver detects dropped packets only when a subsequent sequence gap\noccurs; the end of a data stream may require additional local\ndiagnostics. The case study records all losses at the source and does\nnot simulate bit errors on the line.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must a timeout not be transmitted as a measured value of\nzero?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Zero may be a valid physical measurement.\nStatus and value must have distinct meanings.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere is the write index?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(14+5)\\bmod16=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the sync word insufficient for robust framing?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> The two bytes can appear by chance in the\npayload; length, CRC and sequence checks provide additional criteria,\nbut not absolute proof of uniqueness.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a full buffer not automatically a sensor fault?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> The sensor may operate correctly while the\ntransport rate or a blocked output overloads the queue.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://users.ece.utexas.edu/~valvano/EE445M/lectures.html\">University\nof Texas at Austin – Embedded and Real-Time Operating Systems, lecture\noverview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Spring 2026, weeks 1 and 3: modular software, thread\nstates and synchronization; not a direct finite-state-machine text. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ee.ethz.ch/studies/bachelor/bsc-eeit/third-year/core-courses.html\">ETH\nZürich – Core Courses and Specialisations (Bachelor)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Core Courses:\nEmbedded Systems, Computers and Networks; curricular comparison, not\nevidence of the specific semester offering. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M15-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M15-L5 – Sensor Node Case Study<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Embedded Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Verify measurement requests,\nbuffers, fault packets, timestamps and transport deadlines in a\nreproducible event model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Task and Criteria<\/h2>\n<p>The sensor node is to initiate 200 periodic read requests at 100 Hz\nover 2 s. It has 8 KiB of SRAM (budget from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L1<\/a>)\nand transmits twelve bytes per request. The normal path takes 600 µs\nfrom the timer event to the completed packet; every request whose\nsequence number satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>37<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\bmod37=17<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead produces a timeout packet after 1600 µs. Requests are numbered\nfrom 0 to 199. This introduces five deliberate sensor faults. The test\nrequires no lost packets, no violation of the 2 ms acquisition deadline\nand no packet whose transmission ends more than 150 ms after the\nscheduled timer instant. Fault packets count as successfully transmitted\ndiagnostics, not as valid measurements.<\/p>\n<p>Two UART rates (9600 and 19200 bit/s, 8N1), different FIFO capacities\nand an artificial transmission pause are compared. The pause blocks new\ntransmission starts from 200 ms up to but not including 320 ms; packets\nalready being transmitted finish normally. It represents, for example,\nan abstract flow-control block between packets. Acquisition continues.\nAfter the final measurement request, the buffer is allowed to drain\ncompletely. This drain is necessary so that packets still waiting are\nnot incorrectly counted as lost.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible Event Simulation<\/h2>\n<p>Simulated time is expressed in integer microseconds. The event queue\nis sorted by timestamp and, in the event of a tie, by event type:\ntransmission completion, end of the pause, then acquisition completion.\nFor each append, FIFO occupancy is checked for a peak before any\nimmediate transmission start; a packet in the transmitter itself\noccupies a separate memory slot. CPU times, arrival of bus events and\nthe UART model are deterministic assumptions, not hardware measurements.\nIn the simulation, <code>deque<\/code> replaces the ring demonstrated in\nM15-L4; the capacity limit and FIFO semantics remain the same.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> heapq, struct<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> collections <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> deque<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> crc8(data):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    crc<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> data:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        crc <span class=\"op\">^=<\/span> b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            crc<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>((crc<span class=\"op\">&lt;&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">^<\/span><span class=\"bn\">0x07<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">255<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> crc<span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">128<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> (crc<span class=\"op\">&lt;&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">255<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> crc<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> packet(seq, t):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    fault<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>seq<span class=\"op\">%<\/span><span class=\"dv\">37<\/span><span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">17<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    adc<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bn\">0xffff<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> fault <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>seq<span class=\"op\">%<\/span><span class=\"dv\">200<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    data<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>struct.pack(<span class=\"st\">&#39;&lt;2sHIHB&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">b&#39;<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\xaa\\x55<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;<\/span>,seq,t,adc,<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(fault))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> data<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"bu\">bytes<\/span>([crc8(data)])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> simulate(baud, capacity, pause<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">200<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    duration<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">120_000_000<\/span><span class=\"op\">//<\/span>baud <span class=\"co\"># us, exact for selected baud<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> duration<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>baud<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">120_000_000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    events<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>deque()<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> done<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> dropped<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> trace<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> peak<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> busy<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> seq <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(n):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>seq<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        data<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>packet(seq,t)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        ready<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>t<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1600<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> data[<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>] <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">600<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        heapq.heappush(events,(ready,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,seq,data))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> pause: heapq.heappush(events,(<span class=\"dv\">320000<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">b&#39;&#39;<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> events:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        t,kind,s,data<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>heapq.heappop(events)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> kind<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>: <span class=\"co\"># UART completed<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            busy<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> done.append((s,t<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10000<\/span>,data))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">elif<\/span> kind<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(q)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>capacity: dropped.append(s)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>: q.append((s,data))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span>peak<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(peak,<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(q))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> busy <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> q <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> (pause <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">200000<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>t<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">320000<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            s,data<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>q.popleft()<span class=\"op\">;<\/span>busy<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            heapq.heappush(events,(t<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>duration,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,s,data))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        trace.append((t,<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(q)))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(done)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(dropped)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>n<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> [s <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> s,_,_ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> done]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sorted<\/span>(s <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> s,_,_ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> done)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(crc8(data[:<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>data[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _,_,data <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> done)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-42\"><a href=\"#cb1-42\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> peak<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>capacity<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-43\"><a href=\"#cb1-43\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">dict<\/span>(sent<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(done),drop<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(dropped),peak<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>peak,late<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(delay<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">150000<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _,delay,_ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> done),max_delay<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(delay <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _,delay,_ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> done),fault<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(data[<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _,_,data <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> done),last<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(s<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10000<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>delay <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> s,delay,_ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> done),trace<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>trace)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-44\"><a href=\"#cb1-44\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span><span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;__main__&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-45\"><a href=\"#cb1-45\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;CRC&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">hex<\/span>(crc8(<span class=\"st\">b&#39;123456789&#39;<\/span>)),packet(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>).<span class=\"bu\">hex<\/span>())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-46\"><a href=\"#cb1-46\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> c <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">19200<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">19200<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">19200<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">9600<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">9600<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">32<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-47\"><a href=\"#cb1-47\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(c,{k:v <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k,v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> simulate(<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>c).items() <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k<span class=\"op\">!=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;trace&#39;<\/span>})<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Transmission duration is calculated from the 120 bit periods; the\nselected rates divide 120 million exactly. Other bit rates would require\na finer or rational time representation. Time is measured relative to\nthe request instant, not packet completion. The code checks sequence\norder, CRC and conservation of the number of generated records. Checking\nthe CRC of an unchanged packet is a consistency test, not a test of an\nactual wireless or wired link.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Results<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-8\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Bit/s<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">FIFO slots<\/th>\n<th>Pause<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Sent<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Dropped<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Peak (slots)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Late (sent)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. delay (ms)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Fault packets sent<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">19200<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">200<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.85<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">19200<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">200<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">126.25<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">19200<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">192<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">126.25<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9600<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">176<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">121<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">213.10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9600<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">32<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">182<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">18<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">32<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">166<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">412.50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>All read requests in the model complete as a record or fault\ndiagnostic within 1600 µs, meeting the acquisition deadline. With 16\nslots and 19200 bit/s, the sensor node also meets all transport criteria\nduring the defined pause. Peak occupancy of 12 slots leaves four\nadditional queue slots free. Transmission of the final packet ends after\n1.99685 s in this configuration. A four-slot buffer saves 144 bytes but\ndrops eight packets. Having no late transmitted packets does not mean\nthat the entire test succeeded here.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_056_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Queue occupancy over time for three combinations of UART rate, capacity and transmission pause.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The simulated queue responds to UART rate, buffer\ncapacity and a transmission block; a higher peak occupancy need not\ncause losses as long as capacity suffices.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Design and Limits of Verification<\/h2>\n<p>At 9600 bit/s the data rate is limited to 80 packets/s; the\ngeneration rate remains 100 packets/s. In the steady-state model, a\nbacklog of 20 packets per second therefore develops as long as no data\nis discarded. Larger buffers delay the first overflow, but may also\nincrease the maximum transport time. With 19200 bit/s, 160 packets/s are\navailable, providing a reserve of 60 packets/s. With twelve packets\nwaiting, the time to clear the backlog can be estimated as approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12/60=0{.}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The exact evolution depends on event phase, the existing transmitter\nstate and release times.<\/p>\n<p>For the 16-slot design, the SRAM budget is 4384 bytes; 3808 bytes\nremain unallocated. The CPU portions of the read requests are budgeted\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">200\\cdot100\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms in 2 s (1%). UART servicing, other interrupts, RTOS, bus recovery and\nactual stack peaks are not simulated separately. The budget is therefore\nnot a measured worst-case execution time. In actual commissioning,\ntimer/IRQ latencies would be tested with a logic analyser or tracing,\nalong with the stack high-water mark and map file, bus faults, repeated\ninterruptions, receiver parser and counter wraparound. The timing\naccuracy of the sensor converter itself must also be verified\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At 50 Hz, 9600 bit/s and 12 bytes, is the average baud rate\nsufficient without pauses?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 6000 bit/s, corresponding to 62.5% of the\nnominal data rate. Deadlines must also be checked individually.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which configuration passes the defined criteria even with the single\n120 ms pause?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 19200 bit/s with 16 FIFO slots. 200 records\nsent, no losses or deadline violations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the 9600 bit/s variant with 32 slots and a pause exhibit\n166 late packets despite the larger buffer?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Its throughput is permanently insufficient;\nthe larger queue allows more old packets to be retained, and the pause\nincreases the backlog further.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Change <code>capacity<\/code> to 12 and 11 in the pause case at 19200\nbit/s.<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> At 12, all 200 are sent; at 11, one packet is\nmissing. This limit applies exactly to the event phases defined here; 12\nwould be without reserve.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> M16 explores the physical measurement\nchain and digital measurement signal processing in greater depth. The\nsensor node here concerns raw-value transport, not sensor calibration\nyet.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://users.ece.utexas.edu/~valvano/EE445M/lectures.html\">University\nof Texas at Austin – Embedded and Real-Time Operating Systems, lecture\noverview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · ECE445M, lecture schedule week 9: sensor processing and\ninput capture; slides not reviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ee.ethz.ch/studies/bachelor/bsc-eeit/third-year/core-courses.html\">ETH\nZürich – Core Courses and Specialisations (Bachelor)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Core Courses:\nEmbedded Systems, Computers and Networks; curricular comparison, not\nevidence of the specific semester offering. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/user-guide/UM10204.pdf\">NXP –\nUM10204, I²C-bus specification, Rev. 7.0, §§ 3.1, 6, 7.2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · UM10204,\nRev. 7.0, p. 9, §3.1.4–3.1.5: start/stop conditions and byte format;\npartial scope. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onlinedocs.microchip.com/oxy/GUID-D5344754-46CE-40D6-918B-245670AF7E39-en-US-6/GUID-0DB53D39-B94A-4512-95BD-D6BA10D41F83.html\">Microchip\n– USART Overview, asynchronous operation and 8N1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2 Overview:\nasynchronous 8N1 frame and baud rate; device-specific manual requires\nseparate review. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
